# tramp_sys.redefs - the six file system calls, renamed to the trampolines of
# tramp_sys.c (the in-memory file and the system-call failure schedule).
#
# build.sh gives this map as OV_DSL_LIBRARY_REDEFS, so oss-verify's dsl.py
# (docs/build-contract.md, "DSL fuzz targets") applies it, in the same
# llvm-objcopy call as the allocation map, to a DERIVED copy of the library
# archive, lib/liboft.a, and to nothing else: not to the driver objects, cli.o,
# libc.a, libc++, libunwind or the sanitizer runtime. dsl.py refuses a driver
# object that names either side of a pair. cli.c reads the input with the real
# open/read/close before the program runs, so the driver's own input reading
# cannot fail, and musl's stdio reaches the kernel through its internal
# syscall wrappers, not these names, so the driver's stdout cannot either.
#
# Format: one `old new` pair per line; `#` starts a comment.
#
# DELIBERATELY ABSENT: open64, lseek64, fcntl64, pread, pwrite, readv, writev,
# creat, openat. zlib 1.4 at the pinned commit references none of them in any
# build (under musl, gzlib.c:12-16 picks lseek). dsl.py checks that each pair
# here moves every reference, and its census refuses a library copy that
# still calls a name that the copy does not define, that is no new name of a
# map, no sanitizer's, and no line of sysfail_fuzzer.sys.allow: a later zlib
# that calls one of these fails the build instead of reaching the kernel.
open tramp_sys_open
read tramp_sys_read
write tramp_sys_write
lseek tramp_sys_lseek
close tramp_sys_close
fcntl tramp_sys_fcntl
